From: Kettle Linux
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fastrpc: a freezable wait for user invocations

A user invocation waits for the DSP's answer in an interruptible sleep.
Every suspend interrupts it: the freezer's fake signal ends the wait
with -ERESTARTSYS, the context stays on the pending list, and after
resume the system call is restarted and sends the same message to the
DSP again.

hexagonrpcd (the sensors' reverse-RPC listener) waits in such a call
nearly all the time, and its message carries the reply to the DSP's
previous request. After a suspend the DSP gets that reply twice, and
its listener asserts on the next request: on the AYN Odin 3 Portal,
with a game starting the sensors a while after a few suspends, the
ADSP's qsh_process died with "listener_hexagon.c:326: Assertion
prevCtx == notify failed", and the ADSP restart that followed hung
the SoC.

Sleep TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE | TASK_FREEZABLE: the freezer freezes the
caller in place and nothing is sent again. A real signal still ends
the wait as before.
---
--- a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
@@ -1328,7 +1328,16 @@
 		if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&ctx->work, 10 * HZ))
 			err = -ETIMEDOUT;
 	} else {
-		err = wait_for_completion_interruptible(&ctx->work);
+		/*
+		 * Freezable: a suspend freezes the caller here instead of
+		 * interrupting it. An interrupted call returns -ERESTARTSYS
+		 * and is restarted, which sends the message again: for
+		 * hexagonrpcd's listener, a second reply to the same request,
+		 * which the DSP's listener asserts on (qsh_process
+		 * "listener_hexagon.c: Assertion prevCtx == notify failed").
+		 */
+		err = wait_for_completion_state(&ctx->work,
+						TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE | TASK_FREEZABLE);
 	}
 
 	return err;
